  "response": "Where the generators and the network operator advance opposing accounts, the question is resolved not by aggregate statistics on the variability of wind and solar output but by reconstructing the disturbance second by second from fault recorders, protection event logs, high-speed SCADA telemetry and the performance standards each plant is registered to meet. Each party's narrative is then required to fit that reconstruction. A severe storm damaged transmission towers in South Australia on 28 September 2016. The turbine protection settings permitted only a limited number of voltage dips before output was cut.\n\nResolving which mechanism was operative therefore begins with the condition of the network and the condition of the plant. At least one of two findings must hold, and conceivably both: the transmission towers were destroyed by tornado-strength winds, or the wind farms' low-voltage ride-through settings were configured below the required capability. The high-speed records then settle what the plant actually did. Voltage ride-through protection settings in the turbines triggered a sustained reduction in wind farm output, and as a result approximately 456 MW of wind generation was lost over about seven seconds. That rapid withdrawal of generation in turn left the Heywood Interconnector carrying more power than its design limit; that is, power flow on the Heywood Interconnector exceeded its rated capacity. Because the flow stood above the rating, the Heywood Interconnector to Victoria tripped after being overloaded.\n\nSouth Australia was electrically separated from the rest of the National Electricity Market. The under-frequency load shedding scheme did not arrest the frequency collapse after the separation. The separation was itself brought about by the overloading and consequent trip of the interconnector, which is why investigators treat the interconnector's transfer limit, rather than resource variability as such, as the hinge of the cascade.\n\nThe market operator stated that the wind farms did not meet their registered generator performance standards. The blackout occurred on 14 February 2017. That finding of non-compliance with the registered standards resulted in the Federal Court imposing penalties exceeding 200 million dollars on the wind farm operators.\n\nThe methodological lesson is that intermittency and planning or protection deficiencies are distinguished by documentary evidence of what each element was configured to do and what it measurably did at each instant, not by the competing characterisations the parties bring to the inquiry.",
